{
  "name": "Sunset",
  "created": "2026-09-13T18:00:00.000Z",
  "notes": "The strip is a sky. A `block` coloured by POSITION lays a gradient along it -- orange at the horizon end through red and rose to violet and indigo -- with `paletteSpan` 0.6, so the strip only ever shows sixty percent of that sky. A sine at 0.0008 Hz slides `paletteOffset` through the other forty: the window starts on the warm end and walks to the cold one, which is the sun going down, then walks back.\n\nAt Speed 50 % one full dusk-and-return takes about 21 minutes; the knob runs it from about 12 minutes to over an hour. Nothing in a patch can read the wall clock, so it begins wherever the sine is when the patch starts -- the phase is set so that is sunset, not midnight.\n\nThe same slow clock sinks the sun: a soft glint near the horizon end that slides further down and fades as the sky cools. Clouds are a noise layer with a black palette blended `multiply`, which dims by its own alpha and so darkens the sky in drifting bands -- the trick `downpour` uses for its gusts.",
